Description: hw/char: Add the Chumby crypto processor
 The Chumby Classic's crypto processor is a microcontroller on a serial
 line that holds the device's identity. Model it as a character device
 answering the requests the stock tools make, with no keys.
 .
 The identity it reports is the GUID ("PIDX", printed by "cpi -p" and
 used as the device ID by the control panel download), the serial
 number ("SNUM", "chumby_version -n") and the hardware version ("HWVR",
 "chumby_version -h"). It is kept in the CP's state, with made-up
 defaults, and chumby_cp_set_identity() lets the board set it.
 .
 The CP is a chardev, not a device, so it registers its migration state
 itself when it is opened: the request received so far, and the queued
 reply bytes with the time each is due on the wire. A restore re-arms
 the delivery timer, so a snapshot taken while the guest's daemon is
 talking to it keeps the exchange going. Lengths and positions are
 checked on load, and the reply's times must not go backwards. A state
 with no section for the CP loads with the CP idle.
